\begin_layout Section
Running Coupling Constants
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In QFT,
 the coupling constants depand on the energy scale.
 (In QFT Course a more detailed info).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The beta function is defined as 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta\left(g\right)=\frac{\partial g }{\partial\ln\mu}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

is the relevant energy scale.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To leading orders we care only on fields that are charged under the symmetry.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For a gauged 
\begin_inset Formula $U\left(1\right)$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|q\right|=1$
\end_inset

 The beta funcion is given,
 to leading order,
 by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\beta\left(g_{1}\right)=\frac{n_{f}g^{3}_{1}}{24\pi^{2}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(This result is often quoted for the case of 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{f}$
\end_inset

Dirac fermion)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For 
\begin_inset Formula $SU\left(N\right)$
\end_inset

 theory ,
 with 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{f}$
\end_inset

 fermion fields the beta function is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\beta\left(g_{n}\right)=\left(\frac{2n_{f}-11N}{3}\right)\frac{g^{3}_{n}}{16\pi^{2}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The sign of the beta function is important because it tells us the relation bewtween the scale of the energy and the coupling constant .
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If coupling goes larger than we lose the ability to make accurate predictions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the case of 
\begin_inset Formula $U\left(1\right)$
\end_inset

 we can use pertubative theroy to make predictions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the 
\begin_inset Formula $SU\left(N\right)$
\end_inset

 if we have low number of fermions we can use pertubative theory to predict observables only at high energy and at low energies pertubativity is lost.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We gonna discuss the implications of this in QCD next.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The coupling constant is determining if we could use pertubation theory.
 It's determining the strength of the interaction.
\end_layout

